# C# Coding Conventions

## Naming
- Use PascalCase for types, methods, properties, and constants
- Use `_camelCase` for private fields
- Use camelCase for parameters and local variables
- Prefix interfaces with `I` (e.g., `IOrderService`)
- Suffix async methods with `Async`

## Nullable Reference Types
- Always enable `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>` in project files
- Annotate nullable return types with `?`
- Use `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull()` for null checks at method entry

## Modern C# Patterns
- Use `record` types for immutable DTOs and value objects
- Use primary constructors for simple dependency injection
- Use switch expressions and is-patterns instead of if/else chains
- Use `var` only when the right-hand side makes the type obvious
- Never use `.Result` or `.Wait()` — always `await`

## Async/Await
- All async methods must accept `CancellationToken cancellationToken = default`
- Forward the cancellation token to all async calls
- Use `ConfigureAwait(false)` in library code

## Error Handling
- Use guard clauses at the top of methods
- Create domain-specific exception types for business errors
- Use `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull()` and `ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty()`

## Documentation
- Add XML doc comments to all public types, methods, and properties
- Use `<paramref name="..."/>`, `<see cref="..."/>`, and `<exception cref="..."/>` tags

## Logging
- Use `ILogger<T>` injected via constructor
- Use message templates (not string interpolation): `logger.LogInformation("Order {OrderId} placed", orderId)`
- Define log messages with `[LoggerMessage]` partial methods for hot paths
